# dd — real-world examples

## Basic read/write with block size and count

```sh
# 512 blocks of 2 bytes each from /dev/urandom into a test file
dd if=/dev/urandom of=/tmp/test.txt count=512 bs=2
```

## Built-in progress (modern coreutils — use this first)

```sh
dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/null bs=128M status=progress
```

## Homelab: write an OpenSUSE Tumbleweed ISO to a USB stick

```sh
# Double-check the device with lsblk first!
lsblk
sudo dd if=openSUSE-Tumbleweed-DVD-x86_64-Current.iso of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress oflag=sync
```

## Homelab: image an SD card before reflashing

```sh
sudo dd if=/dev/mmcblk0 of=~/backups/sdcard-$(date +%Y%m%d).img bs=4M status=progress
```

## Watch progress of an already-running dd

```sh
# Send USR1 to make dd print stats
export dd_pid=$(pgrep '^dd')
while [[ -d /proc/$dd_pid ]]; do kill -USR1 $dd_pid && sleep 1 && clear; done
```

## Progress bars with pv

```sh
# dialog gauge (zypper in pv dialog)
(pv -n /dev/zero | dd of=/dev/null bs=128M conv=notrunc,noerror) 2>&1 \
  | dialog --gauge "Running dd command (cloning), please wait..." 10 70 0

# zenity progress window (zypper in pv zenity)
(pv -n /dev/zero | dd of=/dev/null bs=128M conv=notrunc,noerror) 2>&1 \
  | zenity --title 'Running dd command (cloning), please wait...' --progress
```

## dcfldd — dd fork with progress output built in

```sh
dcfldd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/null bs=500K
```

| Option           | What it does                        |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| if= / of=        | input / output file or device       |
| bs=              | block size (4M is a good default)   |
| count=           | copy only N blocks                  |
| status=progress  | live transfer stats                 |
| conv=noerror     | keep going past read errors         |
| oflag=sync       | flush writes as you go              |
